"""Original introductory social-protection system analysis."""


def build(c, m):
    return c(71, 'Social Protection Systems and Welfare Governance', 'Social Protection Systems\nand Welfare\nGovernance',
        'Analyse a fictional social-protection arrangement for entitlement, effective access, adequate support and accountable delivery without determining real eligibility.', [
        m('Entitlement coverage and adequacy', 'Distinguish policy entitlement from effective access and the adequacy of support.', [
            'A social-protection system combines arrangements that help people manage income insecurity and access essential support across life circumstances. Contributory arrangements generally connect protection to contributions; non-contributory arrangements draw on other public financing. Those labels do not tell us whether a person can actually obtain support. The ILO distinguishes legal and effective coverage, reminding a policy reader to ask both what an arrangement promises and what people receive. Use that distinction when reading the invented programme, without treating a global report as a local eligibility decision. [S51]',
            'Coverage also differs from adequacy. A fictional benefit can reach an eligible group yet be too small, irregular or inaccessible to address its stated purpose. Describe the intended need, the relevant period, the support offered and the remaining gap. Consider complementary services as well as a cash amount: a transport payment cannot create an available clinic appointment. Avoid assuming that the same nominal payment has the same practical value for households facing different access costs or responsibilities. State what information would be needed before a responsible authority could assess adequacy. [S51]',
            'The design of a research comparison matters when considering policy options. A United States trial compared larger and smaller monthly transfers to mothers with low incomes; its findings did not demonstrate broad improvements across every family outcome. That does not establish that income support is useless, or that an invented Nigerian programme would have identical results. It suggests a better question: which outcome is this arrangement intended to influence, through what plausible route, and what other conditions affect that route? Use evidence to refine the question rather than borrow an effect label. [R61]',
            'In an introductory system review, keep entitlement, actual receipt, regularity and adequacy in separate columns. Record the source and date of an invented statement, identify uncertainty and name the role that must verify operating rules. A person missing from an administrative count is not automatically ineligible, and a policy announcement is not proof of payment. The classroom output should explain the differences and propose a bounded next check. It must not decide a real claim, promise a benefit or collect personal histories to fill missing information. [S51]'],
            'An invented allowance is described as universal because its policy covers all eligible residents. A practice report counts only completed payments; some residents cannot reach the enrolment desk, and payment dates vary.',
            'Create four short entries distinguishing entitlement, effective receipt, regularity and adequacy. Identify two unanswered access questions and the responsible verification role.',
            'Legal scope, actual receipt and sufficient reliable support are different. Describe the access gap without deciding individual eligibility or treating completed payments as universal reach.', ['S51', 'R61'], [
                ('Legal scope and effective receipt are distinct.', 'The invented allowance has a broad legal scope but some intended users receive nothing. What distinction is essential?', 'A legal promise differs from effective coverage.', ['A broad legal scope proves all payments occurred.', 'A missing payment proves legal ineligibility.', 'Payment totals alone define every entitlement.'], 'An entitlement statement and evidence of receipt answer different questions.'),
                ('Adequacy concerns support relative to purpose and circumstances.', 'An invented benefit reaches its recipients but arrives too late for its stated purpose. What should the review examine?', 'Timing and practical adequacy as well as reach.', ['Reach alone proves sufficient protection.', 'Nominal payment size settles every access question.', 'Any delay proves intentional misconduct.'], 'Coverage alone does not establish usable, timely support.'),
                ('Complementary service availability affects usable support.', 'A fictional travel allowance is paid while the destination service has no appointments. What remains unresolved?', 'Access to an actually available service.', ['A completed payment proves completed care.', 'The allowance creates clinical capacity automatically.', 'The learner may guarantee an appointment.'], 'A transfer and a functioning service are complementary conditions.'),
                ('Research comparisons must be read in context.', 'A fictional policy note applies the US cash-gift trial directly to every local programme. What correction is strongest?', 'Check the comparison, population, outcomes and transfer limits.', ['A randomised study applies identically everywhere.', 'A journal rank replaces outcome appraisal.', 'One non-significant outcome proves all transfers useless.'], 'Study design supports bounded conclusions, not universal transfer.'),
                ('Uncertain operating rules require qualified verification.', 'A learner finds an invented eligibility statement with no source date. What should they record?', 'The uncertainty and the role that must verify the rule.', ['A binding eligibility decision made by the learner.', 'An invented current legal rule.', 'A real recipient history collected to replace the missing rule.'], 'The exercise analyses a system without creating decision authority.')]),
        m('Delivery access and grievance arrangements', 'Review an invented delivery journey for access barriers, ownership and correction routes.', [
            'A protection policy becomes usable through linked administrative tasks: people learn about support, seek entry, receive an authorised decision and obtain the relevant benefit or service. This is an analytical journey, not a copied operational procedure. At each point, ask what action is required, who has responsibility, what resource is needed and where the process can fail. The ILO discussion of gaps between legal and effective protection supports attention to these implementation conditions. A well-written policy cannot compensate for a payment channel or service desk that does not work. [S51]',
            'Access barriers can accumulate. An information notice may assume literacy, a registration route may require repeated travel, and a payment arrangement may depend on a device that the intended user cannot use privately. A single digital channel can help some people while excluding others. Examine a concrete barrier before proposing an adjustment; do not presume that everyone in a demographic group needs the same support. OECD participation guidance also emphasises access, resources and appropriate tools. In the invented journey, compare feasible alternatives and identify which responsible role can authorise or resource them. [S53]',
            'A correction route needs more than a contact address. It requires a clear purpose, an accessible way to raise a concern, a responsible recipient and an honest explanation of what can happen next. Different matters may require different authorised arrangements: a missing payment, an eligibility dispute and an urgent protection concern should not be silently treated as the same routine enquiry. Do not promise absolute confidentiality or a universal response deadline. State what qualified local procedures would need checking, while using only invented administrative facts in the classroom exercise. [S51, S53]',
            'Review should connect the journey to evidence of action. A message sent is not necessarily received; a referral recorded is not necessarily accepted; and a complaint closed in a spreadsheet is not necessarily resolved for its user. Keep a versioned account of the fictional issue, response owner, proposed correction and next review point. Where a partner delivers part of the service, specify how responsibilities connect rather than assume the handover removes accountability. This develops administrative reasoning; it is not a completed government review, public consultation or professional case investigation. [S54]'],
            'An invented programme has an app-only registration route and one complaint inbox. No role checks the inbox during leave; an email referral is marked as support delivered.',
            'Map three distinct failures, a feasible conditional correction and a responsible role for each. Distinguish sending, receiving and actual delivery in the referral entry.',
            'Offer access alternatives, assign covered response responsibility and verify the actual handover. Preserve unresolved conditions instead of declaring a completed service from an email.', ['S51', 'S53', 'S54'], [
                ('Administrative tasks need named responsibility.', 'An invented registration step has instructions but no assigned owner. What is missing?', 'Responsibility and capacity to perform and review the task.', ['A longer publicity slogan.', 'Proof that delivery is already complete.', 'A reason to transfer all responsibility to the applicant.'], 'An operational journey depends on accountable tasks and resources.'),
                ('Digital access is not universal access.', 'An invented app-only benefit process excludes users without suitable devices. What is a defensible recommendation?', 'Consider a feasible accessible alternative through the authorised programme.', ['Assume excluded users are unwilling.', 'Require public disclosure of personal histories.', 'Declare the app legally sufficient in every locality.'], 'An access adjustment should respond to the specific barrier.'),
                ('Different concerns may require different authorised routes.', 'A fictional inbox receives a payment enquiry and a serious concern outside its routine scope. What should the review distinguish?', 'Routine correction responsibilities and designated qualified arrangements.', ['Every matter must follow the same unverified deadline.', 'The learner should investigate both cases independently.', 'Closing the inbox resolves both concerns.'], 'Scope and appropriate responsibility matter when issues differ.'),
                ('Referral transmission does not establish delivery.', 'An invented spreadsheet says a service was delivered because an email was sent. Which status is supportable?', 'Referral sent; acceptance and service delivery remain unverified.', ['Support fully received and effective.', 'An authorised decision already made by the learner.', 'Guaranteed service capacity.'], 'Records should distinguish stages and preserve uncertainty.'),
                ('Correction requires traceable follow-through.', 'An invented complaint is marked closed without reasons or a response owner. What improves accountability?', 'A clear status, reasons, responsible role and next review point.', ['Deleting the original concern from the history.', 'Using fewer complaints as proof of fairness.', 'Publishing identifying records as a transparency measure.'], 'A traceable response is more informative than administrative closure alone.')]),
        m('Review system performance and evidence', 'Interpret fictional protection indicators without conflating activity, access and effects.', [
            'Performance questions should follow the policy purpose. Applications received, decisions completed and payments issued describe administrative activity or outputs. They may be useful, but they do not by themselves show that a household experienced sufficient income security or could use an essential service. Choose an invented indicator with a clear definition, denominator, period and responsible source. If the relevant population is uncertain, disclose the limitation. A dashboard should help people understand the question rather than replace it with an attractive total. [S51, S55]',
            'Use research to separate a plausible mechanism from an established outcome. The Brazilian cohort study of people with recorded violence exposure found an association between programme participation and some mortality outcomes. It was observational, used older administrative records and could not isolate every complementary intervention or conditionality. Its design therefore cannot establish that conditional transfers outperform unconditional transfers everywhere. The US randomised study asked different questions in a different population. Combining the studies into a single universal ranking would hide those differences. [R62, R61]',
            'Fictional monitoring can expose unequal access even when aggregate activity rises. Suppose completed payments increase while travel-related failures remain concentrated in a remote area. The overall count does not settle whether the programme meets its inclusion purpose. Compare relevant groups using appropriate invented denominators, acknowledge missing records and examine plausible implementation explanations. Do not infer motive or corruption from a disparity alone. Identify what an authorised review would need to investigate, and keep a proposed administrative adjustment distinct from a demonstrated improvement in wellbeing. [S51, S54]',
            'A useful review ends with proportionate decisions. State what the evidence supports, what remains uncertain, which action is proposed and who must authorise and revisit it. It may be appropriate to improve a response route while gathering stronger outcome evidence through a separately governed evaluation. The learner should not collect recipient histories or reproduce a study instrument to manufacture confidence. A completed fictional review shows reasoning about protection systems; verified assessment results and the academy’s separate credential requirements determine recognition. [S55]'],
            'An invented report combines higher payment counts, a positive cohort association and a US trial into a claim that its local programme has eliminated insecurity and that one transfer design is always superior.',
            'Write a bounded performance conclusion separating activity, access, adequacy and effects. Explain why the two studies cannot establish that universal ranking.',
            'Counts show recorded activity, not the elimination of insecurity. Compare study questions and designs, disclose uncertainty and propose an authorised review of specific delivery gaps.', ['S51', 'S55', 'R62', 'R61', 'S54'], [
                ('Activity indicators are distinct from wellbeing outcomes.', 'The fictional report counts payments and declares insecurity eliminated. Which correction is justified?', 'Payment activity needs separate evidence about access, adequacy and outcomes.', ['Every recorded payment proves wellbeing improvement.', 'More payments prove all intended users were reached.', 'A dashboard total makes the denominator irrelevant.'], 'An administrative output does not measure every policy objective.'),
                ('Observational estimates retain confounding limits.', 'An invented note calls the Brazilian cohort a randomised comparison of transfer designs. What is accurate?', 'It estimates associations in a selected administrative cohort.', ['It randomly assigns conditional and unconditional programmes worldwide.', 'It removes all unmeasured confounding.', 'It directly predicts the fictional programme effect.'], 'Weighting and sensitivity analyses do not change an observational design into a trial.'),
                ('Different studies do not automatically rank policy designs.', 'The fictional analyst compares US family outcomes with Brazilian mortality associations to rank all transfer designs. What is needed?', 'Recognise different populations, comparisons, outcomes and settings.', ['Rank programmes solely by publication date.', 'Treat every outcome as interchangeable.', 'Choose the study with the longest title.'], 'A policy comparison requires compatible questions and defensible evidence.'),
                ('Aggregate growth can conceal unequal access.', 'Invented payment totals rise while one area has repeated travel barriers. What should be reviewed?', 'Relevant access differences and denominators alongside the total.', ['The total proves inclusion in every area.', 'The difference proves individual misconduct.', 'Omit the area because the overall number improved.'], 'Disaggregated implementation questions can reveal gaps hidden by totals.'),
                ('Recommendations need honest status and authority.', 'A fictional system review proposes a new response channel but no approval exists. How should it be labelled?', 'A conditional recommendation with an owner and review point.', ['A completed and proven reform.', 'A binding decision issued by the learner.', 'An automatic entitlement change.'], 'A defensible review preserves the distinction between proposal and implementation.')])])
